            <ArticleTitle>Emergency gastrointestinal surgery in the elderly.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S73-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jviscsurg.2015.09.016</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1878-7886(15)00123-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The main problem in management of elderly patients who present to the emergency department with abdominal pain is related to difficulties in establishing a diagnosis, because of frequently impaired communication as well as to unusual clinical and laboratory presentations, resulting in delayed management. Early use of pertinent imaging may reduce this delay. Surgical procedures in the elderly do not differ from those in younger patients, but their associated morbidity is different. Assessing co-morbidities and patient frailty, as well as taking into consideration the diagnosis, patients' wishes and status should help in decision-making. Therapeutic decisions should involve surgeons, anesthesiologists and geriatricians alike, both pre- and postoperatively, with the goal of optimizing patients' rehabilitation and offering good and appropriate care while ensuring the humane, social and financial aspects.</AbstractText>
